WEBVTT

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.250
So

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.350
this first section is called Verbal Idioms.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.750
Now we're getting into micro studies.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.280
The first section is called--yeah sorry

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:20.270
This first section is called Verbal Idioms.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:22.160
And

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.050
let me explain what an idiom is first, and we'll discuss a few examples of them.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:27.320
There's lots of them in the notes,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.890
but I'll just give you a few of them

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.060
the ones that are particularly interesting Inshallah

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.460
and that should give you an idea.

00:00:33.931 --> 00:00:36.150
In any language

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:41.160
there is a way of saying something directly like you say, for example, I gave

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:42.650
I gave.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.360
But there's a difference between saying I gave and I gave up

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.130
because when you say I gave that means I gave time, I gave money, I gave a gift,

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.090
I gave a present,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.250
etcetera. But when you say I gave up, what does that mean?

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.160
Can you give it? Can you say it In other words in English? I gave up.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:01.650
I quit

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:03.260
I quit.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:05.640
In other words, you had the word 'gave'

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.700
if you look up the meaning of gave in the dictionary,

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.360
it will say something about giving something.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.000
I don't know how they define it.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.760
The act of contributing or something.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.060
But when you say gave up, did the meaning change entirely?

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.460
Sure, sure.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.220
Similarly, even a single word, for example, you have the word site,

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.060
S I T E. Site.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.080
If you talk to a construction worker, he's thinking of the construction site.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.160
If you talk to a Web developer, he's thinking of the

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:35.560
website.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.360
You understand?

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.550
Similarly, you have a word like journal

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.260
journal, if you talk to an accountant, he's thinking of the accounting journal.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.060
If you're talking to an academic, he's thinking about the academic journal.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.650
If you're talking to a story writer or a 14 year old girl,

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.100
she's thinking about a personal journal.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:54.160
Dear diary,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.860
Today was a wonderful day,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:57.750
You know?

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.450
So 'journal' can mean different things to different people.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:03.330
In other words,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.660
you cannot just learn language by looking words up in a dictionary and saying,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:07.840
This is what this word means.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.810
Every word means one thing. Language is alive. It's dynamic.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.690
It's used different ways. There are figures of speech, and that's the other thing.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.160
You can say verbal idioms are a kind of figure of speech,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.150
so let me give you an example of an English figure of speech.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.680
You know when something is really awesome, we say in English,

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:25.960
sometimes say it's cool,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:27.760
it's cool.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.130
But as the language changes, I'm not keeping up anymore,

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:36.110
but there are other ways of saying 'It's cool'. It's popping, It's cracking. It's,

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.950
you know, whatever. They have different ways of saying it,

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.150
and every 10 years it completely changes.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.260
It completely changes.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.960
But if you don't know the expression 'that's cool'

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.660
and you walk into Malaysia, you get to the airport.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.320
You look at the palm tree and says, That's cool.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.760
And the cab driver says, No, actually, it's hot.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.800
Because that's a figure of speech; I'm not talking about the temperature.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:07.990
I'm just saying, This is nice.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:09.360
You understand?

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.400
So sometimes the words are not--you cannot take them literally. In any language

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.960
sometimes you cannot take the words literally. You have to take them figuratively.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.350
Similarly, when I say, What's up?

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.060
What's up?

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.060
What does that mean?

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.430
How are you? What's going on in your life? Etcetera, etcetera. What's up?

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.460
I don't say what's up to you, like, let me check.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.160
Look, you don't do that because that doesn't make any sense,

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:35.380
you understand?

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:37.660
But you would not know that if somebody memorized

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.650
the whole dictionary and then came to New York,

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.960
they know the word 'What' they know the word 'is' and they know the word 'up'

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.280
Now they land at the airport, they go to the taxi driver. He looks at him.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:48.960
What's up, man?

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.010
He goes, No, I was up. I already landed.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:53.340
Uh huh.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:54.650
You know?

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.460
It's not gonna work.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.240
So the Qur'an actually from back in the day, from the time of the Arabs,

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.160
even before Islam, they had some figures of speech.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.260
They had some expressions

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.360
and they used them a lot,

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:10.850
and Allah used them in the Qur'an.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:12.960
This is important now.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.160
They were they were already using it

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:16.750
and Allah used it too.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.060
In other words, if you really want to understand what Allah is saying,

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.060
you have to understand how they were using it before.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.550
You get me--you get what I'm saying?

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.160
So here's the first one. It's called coolness of the eyes.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:31.940
قُرّةُ أَعْيُن

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:34.690
Coolness of the eyes.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.960
What in the world does coolness of the eyes mean? Literally you can take a banana peel

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:42.550
and

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:44.410
cool your eyes,

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:50.500
But the Arabs actually used coolness of the eyes and warmth of the eyes

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.960
back in the day. Let me explain what they mean.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.550
They would say ( اسخن الله عينه ) may Allah roast or may Allah warm his eyes.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.160
They used to say that when they cursed someone.

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:07.650
I hope you you cry so much out of sadness

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.750
that your eyes become warm.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:16.160
I hope Allah warms your eyes, means I hope you shed tears of sorrow.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.900
We don't say that nowadays, I hope Allah warms your eyes.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:21.420
Warms your eyes,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.160
Warms my eyes? I'm going to stand next to a microwave? What do you want me to do?

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.270
But warms your eyes means may you suffer sadness.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.260
May you shed tears of sorrow. That's what that means.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.350
The opposite of warming the eyes is what?

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.180
Cooling the eyes.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:38.360
Now

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.490
let me tell you what cooling the eyes means,

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.610
and I'll compare warming the eyes and cooling the eyes.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.550
Cooling the eyes actually means shedding tears of joy.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:52.220
When you're shedding tears of joy, you're so happy you're crying?

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:53.950
That's called cooling the eyes.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.950
When you're so sad you're crying that's called?

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:59.860
Warming the eyes. So let me give you a comparison.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:02.460
You go to the airport and there are two mothers.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:05.310
There are two mothers, one mother is at the departure

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:08.060
terminal. One mother is at the arrival terminal.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:10.460
One mother, her son is coming back.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:12.460
One mother, her son is?

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:14.560
Leaving. They're both crying.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.260
Which mothers' eyes are cool?

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:21.450
The arrivals mother.

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:24.360
She sees her son. She cries. But these are tears of what?

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.400
Joy. Tears of happiness.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:28.410
The mother who's saying goodbye to her son.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:30.060
Her eyes are what?

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.480
Warm because those are tears of sadness.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:37.890
You understand that the idea of coolness of the eyes being tears of joy,

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:38.870
tears of happiness.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.250
That's number one, and we'll come back to this idea. But there's more to it.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:43.460
There's more.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:48.050
The Arabs would say that when they are traveling in the desert by the way when

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:49.330
the Arabs traveled in the desert

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:50.870
they wore a lot of heavy clothes,

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.450
and you would think, Why are they wearing heavy clothes?

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:56.050
It's a desert, but actually this was a way of protecting themselves.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.100
Sometimes heavy clothes are the best protection against extremely hot weather,

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.750
and they would wrap up their entire face too; their turbans were huge.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:05.520
They were used to wrap up their whole face.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.250
The only part they could not wrap up was the

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:09.220
eyes.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.860
Now there's a sandstorm.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.420
When there's a sandstorm, you have to wrap up your face,

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.800
but you cannot afford to cover your eyes.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.670
The camel does not have that problem; Allah created the camel in an amazing way.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.450
It's eyelid traps the sand and drops it down like a wiper system.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:27.460
Like you know, it does that. That's that's Allah's creation,

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.950
you know? But the human being doesn't have that.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:33.140
So the sand is hitting the eyes and his eyes are burning.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.540
And he used to say that my eyes are warmed and finally

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.230
he finds some kind of cave or a dune or something,

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.640
and he gets some relief.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.750
And he says, Finally, my eyes have become

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.010
cold. In other words,

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:49.760
eyes becoming cold is also used-- or cool eyes was also used to find relief.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:56.760
The first meaning of cool eyes is tears of joy. The second meeting is what?

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:58.180
Relief.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.160
Relief. Like you're in a storm and you finally found

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:01.860
relief.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:07.400
Similarly, a poet used to use my-- like back in the day

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.540
I know some of you are video game people so you know assassin's creed and stuff.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.540
But back in the day, they used to have assassins.

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:16.510
Every tribe had assassins, people that are used to kill others.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:20.650
So this tribe has an assassin and they sent him to kill the enemy

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:24.390
to kill the leader of the other tribe because the tribe attacked them.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.520
And they want revenge. So they sent their assassin guy to kill the other guy.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:30.260
And this assassin is also a poet,

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:34.700
so he makes poetry, and he basically says, I'll make it simple for you,

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:38.659
he basically says, My tribe's eyes will stay warm

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.549
and the blood of my enemy will cool them.

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:47.550
So when I kill my enemy, their eyes will finally become what?

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:50.460
Cool, meaning they'll finally feel relieved

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:55.860
like their anger and their sadness will go away when I kill that guy.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.390
So I I have the job of cooling my tribe's eyes

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:01.860
by killing that guy. That's what he's saying.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:02.860
So

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:05.950
relief and what, tears of?

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:06.760
Joy.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:09.490
Allah 'Azzawajal says in the Qur'an

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:13.400
رَبَّنَا هَبْ لَنَا مِنْ أَزْوَٰجِنَا وَذُرِّيَّٰتِنَا

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:15.760
قُرَّةَ أَعْيُنٍۢ

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:18.300
Allah says in the Qur'an

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:21.570
Ya Allah, oh Allah give us from our spouses and our children

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:23.560
the coolness of the eyes.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:25.660
What in the world does that mean?

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:30.860
That means that our spouses and our children are supposed to be a refuge from a storm.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:36.290
The outside world is a storm. It's full of problems. It's full of stress.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:37.650
It's full of difficulty.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.450
And when I come back to my wife,

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:43.860
when the wife comes back to the husband and the children, then finally they have what?

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.760
Refuge. They feel safe.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.450
The storm cannot hit them anymore,

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:52.950
and it also-- and so they have refuge, they have protection.

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.960
It's kind of sad because for a lot of people the storm is not

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.370
outside the house.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:00.760
The storm is

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:02.720
inside the house.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.640
And so when the storm is inside the house,

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:09.230
your eyes stay warm inside the house and the only way

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:11.070
you can cool them is to get out of the house.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:14.630
And so Allah is teaching me and you a Du'a: Ya Allah

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:20.170
I want a house that when I go back, I am relieved. I want peace in my house.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:22.950
I don't want fighting in my house. I don't want arguments in my house.

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:26.030
I don't want disobedient children that are disrespecting me in my house.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:29.260
I don't want a wife that's always angry. I don't want a husband that's always upset.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:30.736
I want a happy house.

00:10:30.756 --> 00:10:34.830
رَبَّنَا هَبْ لَنَا مِنْ أَزْوَٰجِنَا وَذُرِّيَّٰتِنَا قُرَّةَ أَعْيُنٍۢ

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:37.000
Ya Allah, I don't just want a  happy house.

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:40.670
I want a happy house that is so happy that when I come

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:43.910
home from work and I see the wife and I see the kids,

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:45.960
it makes me so happy that I cry.

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:49.360
That's how happy I want to be.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.450
What a beautiful Du'a, isn't it?

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:57.630
( قرة أعين) tears of joy. Ya Allah, I want tears of joy from my spouse

00:10:57.990 --> 00:10:58.790
and my children.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:00.660
Right now they are tears.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.470
Then you go a little further.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:10.580
Allah 'azzawajal says

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:12.350
that

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:14.060
Musa 'alaihissalam's mother

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:16.460
Musa 'alaihissalam's mother

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.330
when she finally, you know, when she left the baby

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:20.960
and the baby was gone in the water

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:24.290
and her heart was fluttering away and she was in

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.560
deep depression and she didn't know what to do.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:29.160
And finally Allah reunited her with her baby.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.250
And Allah says
كَيْ تَقَرَّ عَيْنُهَا

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:35.360
So her eyes could become

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:36.460
cool.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:41.760
When the baby finally came back she-- last time she saw her baby it was in a river

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:45.350
and then it disappeared. She doesn't know what happened.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:49.370
Next time she sees her baby in the castle and it's crying,

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:51.660
and she can hear the voice of her baby crying.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:54.460
She hasn't even entered the room yet. She can hear the crying,

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:56.550
and she's already starting to cry.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:59.270
What is that cry for, sadness or happiness?

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.380
That's happiness. So she grabs the baby.

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:05.520
And so she's crying and crying and crying because

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.606
she's so happy and Allah says
كَيْ تَقَرَّ عَيْنُهَا

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:13.700
So her eyes could become cool, so she can cry but cry with happiness.

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:16.060
You see the meaning? So beautiful.

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:21.400
This reunion between the mother and child is captured in the coolness of the eyes,

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:23.860
then also

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:26.460
Allah 'azzawajal describes in the Qur'an

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:28.060
Fir'own's wife.

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:29.860
Fir'own's wife.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:32.860
Is she living a happy marriage?

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:34.680
No,

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:37.300
no. If you're confused about that, speak with me later.

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:38.960
She's not in a happy marriage.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:42.980
So Fir'own's wife is terrified of Fir'own

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:45.460
so she's in an abusive relationship.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:49.020
Her husband is psychologically abusive, maybe even physically abusive.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:52.460
We don't know because she did ask Allah to rescue her from him

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:58.131
and she's in a difficult problem because when she asked
نَجِّنِي مِن فِرْعَوْنَ وَعَمَلِهِ

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:01.031
Rescue me from Fir'own and the things that he does.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:02.970
It could mean he does things to her.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:03.960
It's possible

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:07.980
or it could be the things he does to other people. I don't want any part of it.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:11.620
So she's worried about that but she can't call the authorities.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:15.560
She can't call the police against Fir'own because he owns the police.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:18.950
She can't complain to the government because he is the government.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:22.850
She can't run off to another village. He owns all of them.

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:27.020
What's she gonna do? She's stuck. The only one who can rescue her is Allah.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:29.660
So she says
نَجِّنِي مِن فِرْعَوْنَ وَعَمَلِهِ

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:33.420
But why--why am I bringing her up? Because when she discovered Musa,

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:34.440
'alaihissalam

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.160
at the water. She picked him up.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:37.950
She brought him to Fir'own,

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:41.640
and she's scared of Fir'own. We already know this.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.560
She's already terrified of Fir'own, but she found the courage to grab the baby

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:47.450
and bring him to Fir'own.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.860
And this is one of the most amazing movie scenes in the Qur'an.

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:54.950
This woman walks into the court. He's got generals around him.

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:55.960
He's, you know,

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:58.000
because there's a military campaign going on to

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:00.000
kill all the children and she walks in

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.530
with a baby that's supposed to be killed

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:03.410
because his color is different from the Egyptians.

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:07.080
His skin color is different, so he's supposed to be killed right away on site.

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:08.560
But she brings him in any way

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:12.850
and she walks into the court and all the generals like, what's going on?

00:14:13.246 --> 00:14:14.860
There's a baby in the room

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:18.660
and they're discussing killing thousands of babies, and one of them is in the room.

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:20.140
Awkward.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:21.160
You know, like

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:22.160
Uh huh.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:24.850
That's a really strange scene.

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:29.560
And what does she do? She says--first thing she says,
قُرّةُ عَيْنٍ لِي

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:31.350
She says,

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:32.750
This child,

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:34.660
he cools my eye.

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:36.950
He cools my eye.

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.060
I'll give you the third meaning of coolness of the eye.

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:43.660
Qarra (قرة)doesn't just mean to cool.

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:47.290
Qarra (قرة) also means when something stays in one place.

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:50.130
When something stays in one place,

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:52.962
this is why you get the word Qarar (قرار) from this

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:56.160
فَجَعَلْنَٰهُ فِى قَرَارٍۢ مَّكِينٍ When something stays in one place.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:05.220
She is saying to Fir'own, This baby is so cute when I look at him, my eyes stay on him.

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:07.140
I don't look at anything else.

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:09.460
She's stuck on the baby.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:12.260
Some of you that are-- how many people married here?

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:15.710
Married?
إِنَّا لِلّهِ وَإِنَّـا إِلَيْهِ رَاجِعونَ

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:19.070
So you got married and you're-- everything was going great.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:22.470
You love your wife and all of it. Then she was expecting and she had the baby.

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.950
And all of a sudden you felt like an orphan because

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:27.200
every time you come to the wife, what's she doing?

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:32.350
You're like, what about me?

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:34.750
Like

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:37.360
you don't exist when she's holding her baby

00:15:37.591 --> 00:15:40.450
Because her eyes, her eyes stay entirely on

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:41.660
the baby.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:45.250
So she walked in and said, Look at this baby. He's so cute.

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:47.410
I can't even take my eyes off of him.

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:50.950
And then she's also saying at the same time, look at this baby

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:55.770
he's the coolness of my eye because you are the warmth of my eye. I need some coolness.

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:57.960
This is my coolness please,

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:00.060
I need to keep this baby.

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.350
You need to understand my situation. I'm married to you.

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:05.850
I need some relief.

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:07.960
This baby is my relief.

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:09.750
You see the case that she's making?

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:13.410
But then she doesn't stop there. It's so awesome, she says

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:17.450
وَلَكَ and watch this, He can also calm you down.

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:20.710
Why don't you look at him? Can you take your eyes off of him?

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:25.560
So she takes baby Musa 'alaihissalam and sticks him in Fir'own's face

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:31.680
and Fir'own was just discussing what? Killing babies. And he's looking at Musa 'alaihissalam

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:37.520
He's trying not to look. But when he looks when he looks, something happens.

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:41.260
He can't take his eyes off of the baby either.

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:43.060
He got stuck

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:44.760
in the baby's face,

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:46.714
and even he was.

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:51.930
His generals were confused. Everybody's like, What's going on here?

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:57.460
This is, uh, sir, can we discuss killing babies again? Or like

00:16:58.240 --> 00:16:59.260
everything changed.

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:03.170
It's an amazing, amazing scene. And on that note I do want to tell you one more thing.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:05.460
This is part of story night, but who cares? I'll tell you.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:07.349
This is you know

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:10.270
this woman is so smart.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:12.780
She sees

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.819
that Fir'own is looking at the baby

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:17.060
and his face changed.

00:17:17.839 --> 00:17:21.170
You know, when adults look at a cute baby and the condition it is has to be cute.

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:24.300
But if you look at a cute baby, then your face changes.

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:29.210
You're-- even if you're grumpy and you're usually in a bad

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:31.250
mood and all of it when you see a cute baby you just,

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:31.670
you know,

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:34.170
and those of you that are married and have

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:36.770
multiple children and you know your your husband

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:38.670
at least he thinks one of them is cute.

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:40.960
I know it's not fair,

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:45.270
but if you want your husband to do something for you, bring the cute one.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:50.060
I want to go shopping.

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:55.060
Don't just go and say, I want to go shopping.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:58.230
Bring the cute baby

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:00.670
Fir'own's face changed.

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:04.860
Who noticed? The wife noticed, she says
لَا تَقْتُلُوهُ

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:07.360
Okay, now that you're staring at him, don't kill him.

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:11.060
Fir'own says nothing, because he's still lost in the baby.

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:14.990
Then she says, (عَسَىٰ أَن يَنفَعَنَا)  Maybe he could be good to us.

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:17.060
Maybe we could keep him as a servant.

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:21.789
He still didn't say anything. She said, Maybe I should ask some more.
أَوْ نَتَّخِذَهُ وَلَدًا

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:24.450
Maybe we could adopt him as our own--our own kid.

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:26.270
And she--they ended up adopting him,

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:29.260
but all of it because the baby was put in Fir'own's face

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.860
and his eyes, even his his eyes became cool.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:36.350
He lives a life of rage and hate.

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:38.860
Even tyrants are depressed.

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:41.150
Even they're depressed.

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:45.020
And they-- he looks at the baby and he calms down and she gets to negotiate with him.

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:47.210
And a huge part of the negotiation was the

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:50.294
coolness of the eyes that was offered through Musa 'alaihissalam.

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:53.560
Now finally, about coolness of the eyes.

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:55.950
One of the most remarkable

00:18:56.740 --> 00:19:00.178
stories we read in the Qur'an is when Musa 'alaihissalam

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.970
had an opportunity to speak with Allah.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:04.868
Surat at-Taha

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:06.760
Musa 'alaihissalam spoke with Allah.

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:08.450
Now,

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:11.170
when you meet someone important,

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:13.560
you want to remember it forever.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:17.380
There are some 'Ulama that I've had the chance to meet with

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:19.770
in my life for once, one time and they passed away,

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:21.770
and that memory is stuck in my head

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.350
because I got to meet them. It was such an honor that I met them.

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:29.020
There are some people who you never thought you would ever meet in your life,

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:29.850
and you ran into them.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.730
You met them and it becomes a memory you will never, ever, ever forget.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:36.870
Usually you'll try to remember it. If you're in Malaysia, you'll take a picture.

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:38.160
You'll make sure of it. No, no.

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:40.270
If you're in Malaysia, you will take at least three pictures.

00:19:40.923 --> 00:19:42.483
One more.

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:43.860
Wait one more.

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:45.840
Yeah.

00:19:48.723 --> 00:19:50.560
Uh huh.

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:53.820
And all the while, I don't know, you're making a video actually.

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:54.550
But

00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:55.850
but anyway,

00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:00.620
so you you will try to remember that scene and, you know, back in the day,

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:04.200
even our parents and, you know, elders when they met someone important,

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.290
when they shook hands with the president or when

00:20:06.290 --> 00:20:08.910
they graduated or when they met some important person,

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:09.770
they took a picture.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.350
They framed the picture in their house.

00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:14.060
They remind people, Look, I met this person.

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:16.220
They remember it their whole life.

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.550
Actually, whenever you have a conversation with anyone,

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:19.560
you just bring it up.

00:20:20.540 --> 00:20:21.140
By the way,

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:22.160
I met Mufti Menk.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:24.760
You know, just

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:26.270
you slip it in.

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:28.050
You know, that's what I do,

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:30.850
you know?

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:34.040
I know, I know. I don't have much to say, but I've hung out with Mufti Menk

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:36.270
before. Yeah, yeah, really.

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:39.350
He's real, you know? So

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:45.060
so now, having said that Musa 'alaihissalam is speaking with who?

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:47.300
He's speaking with Allah.

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:48.960
He's speaking with Allah.

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:52.170
You're speaking with Allah, you think you'll ever forget?

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:55.260
There's one conversation he will never forget.

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:58.960
You will never speak to anyone more important than Allah Himself.

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:03.720
If there's one memory, all of humanity cannot forget that conversation. How can you Musa 'alaihissalam

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:04.560
forget?

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:07.875
But even Musa was told when he was up on the mountain  'alaihissalam

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:11.360
وَأَقِمِ الصَّلَاةَ لِذِكْرِي Establish Salah so you can really remember Me.

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:14.360
The real way to remember Me will be what?

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:15.860
Salah.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:19.440
I brought that up because there's a really beautiful hadith of the Prophet ﷺ

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:22.430
related to coolness of the eyes that I want you to appreciate.

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:26.636
Rasul ﷺ says
(جُعِلتْ قرةُ عيني في الصلاة)

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:30.770
The coolness of my eyes was put inside the prayer.

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:35.050
The coolness of my eyes was put inside the

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:38.810
prayer. I need you to understand why and why did I bring up Musa 'alaihissalam?

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:40.230
Musa 'alaihissalam

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:42.550
had the most beautiful conversation with Allah

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:45.160
but in that conversation Allah told him to go talk to who?

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:47.170
Fir'own.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:49.580
So he's just had the most beautiful conversation,

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:52.560
and then he's going to have the ugliest conversation ever

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:55.270
because he has to talk to the worst man ever.

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:56.960
Fir'own.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:00.860
The exact opposite, isn't it? You get to talk to Allah, then you have to talk to

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:01.860
Fir'own.

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:08.060
Now the thing is, the conversation with Allah will give you relief.

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:10.200
It will cool your eyes,

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:12.670
but the conversation with Fir'own might

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:15.060
warm your eyes, you understand?

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:18.716
So now Rasulullah ﷺ

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:22.340
all day, what does he have to do? Talk to Quraysh.

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:23.560
Is that easy or hard?

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:28.000
It's very difficult. It gets harder and harder. They get more and more insulting.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:29.270
They get more and more aggressive.

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:31.770
They get more and more disrespectful.

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:36.550
And also, by the way, if you have a clean heart, then you're more sensitive.

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:39.770
So when somebody says bad words to you, it hurts more.

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:43.720
When you are dirty yourself, you use bad words all the time yourself.

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:45.760
When somebody says bad words you're like, bring it,

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:47.060
no problem.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:51.630
But when you're a decent person and you hear foul language, then it really hurts

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:55.890
Rasul ﷺ  hears foul language from them. He hears curses from them.

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:59.260
He hears allegations from them. He gets made fun of by them.

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:04.340
He gets tortured by them every single day and it gets-- doesn't get better the next day

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:07.350
it gets worse the next day and worse the next day and worse the next day.

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:09.630
And it's not like he can do less effort the next day.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:11.670
He has to do even more effort the next day.

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:16.850
He has to keep moving forward. He needs some relief. He's in a storm all the time,

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:19.170
so he says, My relief

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:20.730
from the storm

00:23:20.860 --> 00:23:21.950
was put inside

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:23.360
the Salah.

00:23:24.440 --> 00:23:26.860
It completely changes your perspective

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.450
because you know all the time he's shedding tears of sorrow

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:32.960
now Salah became a time to shed tears of

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:37.060
joy. That's the That's the last thing I want to tell you about قرة عين

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:40.050
I'm spending a lot of time on this one because it's important

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:44.450
the idea that we are supposed to cry in Salat.

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:46.350
It's true,

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:50.690
but usually when you think of crying, you think of sadness.

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:55.450
So people think of crying when they're listening to Ayat about Jahannam.

00:23:58.040 --> 00:23:58.760
But you know what?

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:01.800
The Qur'an should also make you and me cry

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:03.210
not just out of sadness,

00:24:03.350 --> 00:24:05.060
but out of coolness of the eyes,

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:07.170
which means out of joy

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:11.100
when you were listening, reciting Ayat about children.

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:12.950
When you're listening to Ayat about

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:14.770
gifts and Rizq

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:19.400
and what Allah does for you and how He protects you, that should move you to joy,

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:20.590
to tears.

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:21.440
That should happen.

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:25.770
And I pray Allah gives all of us that and lots of it, especially in the month of Ramadan.

00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:27.560
That was the first one.

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:29.060
You guys okay to move on?

00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:30.360
Okay.

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:31.850
Next one.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:35.130
وَٱخْفِضْ لَهُمَا جَنَاحَ ٱلذُّلِّ مِنَ ٱلرَّحْمَةِ

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:38.300
I'll go rather quickly with this one because we have lots and lots of them to do.

00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:42.278
But hopefully you get a taste of you know, these-- It says

00:24:42.268 --> 00:24:47.368
(خفض له جناحه )That's the expression to lower your wings, to lower your wings.

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:51.110
Now the problem with saying this guy lowered his wings is

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:53.160
the first problem is the guy doesn't have wings.

00:24:54.340 --> 00:24:57.270
So what in the world does it mean to lower the wings? When you

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:00.920
say to someone you're lowering your wings or your raising your wings

00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:04.460
رفع له جناحه  خفض له جناحه

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:08.900
They use both of them or (اعلى جناحه) he elevated his wings.

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:10.960
Then you're comparing a person to what?

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:13.860
You're comparing a person to a

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:14.850
bird

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:17.460
you're comparing a person to a bird,

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:19.290
so first understand that comparison.

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:22.060
When a bird raises its wings, what is it about to do?

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:28.050
It's about to fly, when the bird lowers its wings, it's about to land.

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:32.270
So when the bird is going up, it raises its wings. When the bird is going down, it

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:33.550
lowers its wings.

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:36.770
The idea of lowering the wings is: come back down to the earth.

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:39.450
Come back down to the earth. Land down.

00:25:40.183 --> 00:25:45.560
Now obviously, when a bird has wings, it has the ability to raise them anytime it wants.

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:49.950
It has the ability to fly any time it wants, but it still chooses to

00:25:50.500 --> 00:25:54.590
lower them. It's not forced to lower them. It can do it. It's a choice that it makes.

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.720
This is an Ayah about our relationship with our parents.

00:25:57.958 --> 00:26:03.260
وَٱخْفِضْ لَهُمَا جَنَاحَ ٱلذُّلِّ مِنَ ٱلرَّحْمَةِ
lower for them, for your mother and your father

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:04.320
lower for them

00:26:04.330 --> 00:26:08.830
the wings of humility. Allah added the word humility, we'll come back to that

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:11.360
in a second, and do it out of love and mercy for them.

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:12.770
مِنَ ٱلرَّحْمَةِ

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:15.960
Now let's understand this a little. It's very powerful.

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:17.660
When you were little,

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:20.170
they did everything for you.

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:22.950
When a bird is small, can it fly?

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:24.460
No.

00:26:24.940 --> 00:26:27.360
The mother has to go and get all the food

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:30.160
and bring it back to the nest for the chicks, yes?

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:33.170
Because the bird itself does not have what yet?

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:35.790
It doesn't have wings yet. It can't fly on its own yet.

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:40.360
It's completely dependent on the parents. Are we like that when we're babies?

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:44.110
Absolutely. We are completely dependent on our parents.

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:48.610
They clean us, they take us to the bathroom and clean us up. We burp on them.

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:51.839
We throw up on their clothes and they wipe that off and still give us a hug anyway.

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:54.889
We cry over and over again and they calm us down.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:59.350
They give us food, they give us drink, they can protect us. They take us to the doctor.

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:00.950
They give us medicine, they do all of it.

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:01.860
All of it.

00:27:03.610 --> 00:27:05.060
Then we get older.

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:07.270
Then now we're 20

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.170
we're 30 or 40

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:11.360
and they get older too.

00:27:12.540 --> 00:27:13.670
And when they get older,

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:16.860
they get weaker.

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:20.360
But they don't just get weaker. They also get angrier.

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:24.560
And they get also may be hard to deal with for you.

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:28.670
They get they get annoyed very easily.

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:31.170
It takes very little to agitate them.

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:37.670
But now that you're 30 or you're 20 or 25, you have your own job.

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:42.540
You have your own money. You have your own car. You have your own family.

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:46.450
You have your own everything. Which means you can fly on your own.

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:49.770
You can raise your wings, can't you?

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:53.160
Allah says when you're dealing with them

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:55.660
I know you have wings, but you need to what?

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:57.389
Lower them.

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:01.000
and, الذُّلِّ actually means weakness.

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:03.580
In other words, I know that you have powerful wings,

00:28:03.590 --> 00:28:07.260
but when you're with your parents act like you don't have wings at all

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:08.560
like they don't work.

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:13.080
So you don't tell your parents, You know, Dad, I'm an adult now.

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:14.770
You can't talk to me like that anymore.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:17.460
You know, Mother,

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:20.660
I'm in college, okay?

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:26.420
Someone saying that, is raising their wings,

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:27.570
Allah is saying

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:29.930
I got you. I know you got wings.

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:30.920
I know.

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:31.760
Put them down.

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:37.160
The idea is that a bird can fly and it still chooses to stay down.

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:39.770
It still chooses to stay down

00:28:41.140 --> 00:28:43.860
Now, why should you do it? He says
مِنَ الرَّحْمَةِ

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:45.960
Because of mercy.

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:47.890
Because of mercy.

00:28:48.050 --> 00:28:49.710
Which actually means three things.

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:52.560
I'll mention at least three things to you. One

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:57.960
you should have love and mercy for your-- love and care. Remember, الرَّحْمَةِ was love and care?

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:02.860
Because of the love and care they showed you, this is time for you to show love and care.

00:29:04.540 --> 00:29:05.530
This is not--yes

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:09.100
they're annoying and they're loud and they get easily angry and they argue with

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:12.380
you and they don't understand and they still criticize, Why are you doing this?

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:14.730
Why are you driving like that? Why are you sitting like that? Why did you buy that?

00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:16.810
Why are you moving over there? Why are you getting that apartment?

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:19.040
Why did you get that job? Why are you getting married? Why you name your child

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:21.850
that? Why are you-- Why is your child going to that school?

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:23.810
Why? Why? Why? Why? Why? Why? Why?

00:29:23.810 --> 00:29:26.770
And you're like, I can't have the time to explain to you. Okay?

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:28.770
Just let me live my life,

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:30.360
you know?

00:29:32.340 --> 00:29:33.060
But you know what?

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:34.810
A few years ago,

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:36.270
when you were like this

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:38.670
and your mom said

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:41.550
Okay. Time to go to the bathroom. Why?

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:45.050
Time to eat. Why?

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:47.760
Get in the car? Why?

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:53.210
Why can't I have everything? Why do you hate me? Why do you? Why do you? Why do you?

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:55.350
And you used to talk a lot

00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:58.360
and they put up with you.

00:29:59.040 --> 00:30:03.450
What does Allah do as the time goes by? Allah reverses the roles.

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:07.760
He reverses the roles. And actually, that's not just me making that up,

00:30:07.760 --> 00:30:11.560
it's in the Qur'an. The role reversal. I'll explain to you in a second.

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:13.930
The first meaning of (مِنَ الرَّحْمَةِ) is

00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:17.560
the love and care they showed you. Because of that love and care, you should show them

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:18.690
love and care.

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:20.550
The second meaning of (مِنَ الرَّحْمَةِ)

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:24.270
is actually that you,

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:30.260
the-- genuinely are supposed to have love and care for your parents.

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:33.320
And the true meaning of love and care will be when you're humble to them.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:35.850
You cannot raise your voice at your mother and then tell her I love you.

00:30:35.850 --> 00:30:36.960
You know I love you, right, Mom?

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:39.050
But sometimes you're just so bad,

00:30:39.740 --> 00:30:41.260
like, you know yeah

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:44.040
you can't start with,

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:46.170
I love you, Dad, but

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:48.560
can't do that.

00:30:50.440 --> 00:30:52.460
The third meaning is the most powerful to me.

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:56.950
You want Allah's Rahma (رحمة)? You better show them Rahma.

00:30:57.340 --> 00:30:58.770
You better humble your wings.

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:00.480
You better clip your wings

00:31:00.600 --> 00:31:02.270
when you're dealing with your father and your mother

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:06.180
and Allah will sometimes test you by making your parents

00:31:06.590 --> 00:31:08.550
unusually agitating.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:13.760
He will make them say things that they will never say to anyone else

00:31:13.760 --> 00:31:14.660
but they'll say them to you,

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:20.050
and he will make them say things that are so utterly ridiculous

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:26.260
that they will boil your blood. And you will want to say, What are you talking about?

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:29.850
And that will be

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:33.060
the test from Allah on whether or not you lowered your wings.

00:31:34.040 --> 00:31:34.760
It's pretty awesome.

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:39.360
My dad used to do this with me until my mom helped me figure it out.

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:44.670
My dad said the most ridiculous things just to see what I would do.

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:46.770
And I would

00:31:47.440 --> 00:31:48.460
just go crazy.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:51.260
No, Dad, you can't say that. It's not right.

00:31:51.530 --> 00:31:52.860
Mm.

00:31:53.740 --> 00:31:56.770
My dad one time you said, you know, there's this Christian

00:31:57.440 --> 00:31:58.060
preacher.

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:00.350
I listen to him all the time, he is very good.

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:08.550
We're just driving back from the Masjid.

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:11.770
Like there's this Christian guy, I listen to him, he is very good.

00:32:13.040 --> 00:32:16.330
You can't Dad, you can't listen to the Christian dad, its shirk

00:32:16.490 --> 00:32:19.160
You can't Dad, you can't listen to the Christian dad, its shirk -

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:22.770
and he says, You should listen to him. You'll learn something.

00:32:23.840 --> 00:32:25.460
Oh, and then

00:32:25.940 --> 00:32:28.670
my mom pulled me aside and she said,

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:30.360
My stupid son,

00:32:30.940 --> 00:32:31.860
listen carefully,

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:34.950
just go along with it,

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:36.460
agree with it.

00:32:36.910 --> 00:32:37.980
Don't correct him.

00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:38.960
Don't respond,

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:40.270
just go along.

00:32:41.140 --> 00:32:42.690
But I can't. It's wrong.

00:32:42.820 --> 00:32:44.460
and she just, calm down.

00:32:46.840 --> 00:32:47.670
Just do it.

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:50.540
Next time we're driving, he says,

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:54.170
You know that Christian preacher, he's really good. I was like, You know what, dad?

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:56.871
You're right. He is really good. I was listening to him the other day.

00:32:56.790 --> 00:33:00.360
He goes, you better not listen to him. Stop listening.

00:33:03.640 --> 00:33:06.270
It was all a test.

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:10.560
But the beautiful-- the second meaning of lowering the wings.

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:14.770
That's where the beauty is, man. The first meeting is humble yourself. Come down.

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:16.690
Don't raise your wings. Lower them.

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:19.930
I know you know how to fly, but don't fly in front of your parents. Calm down.

00:33:19.940 --> 00:33:21.770
Chill out. Second meaning:

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:25.490
there's a snake, it is about to attack the nest and the

00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:28.110
nest is full of eggs or baby chicks or whatever.

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:32.640
And the mother bird is so scared for her children that it lowers

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:36.900
its wings over the nest and the snake is biting the bird.

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:40.350
But it still doesn't move itself because it's trying to protect what?

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:41.990
Its children.

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:44.130
Lowering the wings for a bird is used as

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:48.260
an expression of the parent sacrificing themselves for the

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:49.460
child.

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:54.210
But the Ayah is not about parents sacrificing themselves for the child.

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:56.060
The Ayah is actually about the children

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:58.160
sacrificing themselves for the

00:33:58.640 --> 00:33:59.150
parent.

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:00.860
You see how the roles got reversed?

00:34:01.240 --> 00:34:03.010
Now it's time for you to be the bird

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:04.450
that lowers the wings.

00:34:05.240 --> 00:34:06.670
They protected you

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:08.550
time for you to protect them.

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:11.560
وَاخْفِضْ لَهُمَا جَنَاحَ الذُّلِّ مِنَ الرَّحْمَةِ

00:34:12.340 --> 00:34:14.050
Which is why the last part of it

00:34:14.440 --> 00:34:17.380
وَقُل رَّبِّ ارْحَمْهُمَا كَمَا رَبَّيَانِي صَغِيرًا

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:19.260
is actually reversal of those roles.

00:34:19.340 --> 00:34:20.500
That's what the Du'a is

00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:24.730
Ya Allah show them love and care like they took care of me and allowed me to grow when I was small,

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:25.460
you know?

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:26.670
Subhanallah

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:27.760
Okay,

00:34:28.739 --> 00:34:31.050
you guys are good to move along one more before I give you a break?

00:34:31.739 --> 00:34:32.969
It's one o'clock already. Wow.

00:34:33.739 --> 00:34:34.460
Time flies.

00:34:35.860 --> 00:34:38.190
It says
وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِّن لِّسَانِي

00:34:39.239 --> 00:34:40.760
Untie the knot

00:34:42.040 --> 00:34:43.469
afflicting my tongue.

00:34:45.040 --> 00:34:47.050
Untie the knot that is hurting my tongue.

00:34:48.639 --> 00:34:49.659
Now a knot

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:51.060
it's not in a tongue.

00:34:51.739 --> 00:34:52.550
Where's a knot?

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:53.850
In a string.

00:34:54.739 --> 00:34:57.260
Shoe strings have a knot. Wires have a knot,

00:34:57.840 --> 00:34:59.060
right? Thread has a knot.

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:02.670
So what does a knot have to do with the tongue? What is this expression about?

00:35:03.740 --> 00:35:04.670
They say,

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:08.970
first of all, when you have-- imagine a string, it's all jumbled up,

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:10.970
right? It's all like

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:13.430
one thing going into the other and to the other

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:14.970
like, you know, your cables.

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:17.550
Do you know how long it is

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:18.850
when you-- when it's like that?

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:22.430
No. Do you know where it begins and where it ends necessarily? No.

00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:25.820
Sometimes three different wires are mixed together.

00:35:25.850 --> 00:35:28.250
You're holding the wire like which one is this one?

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:31.250
You have to untangle it to see which one is which one, right?

00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:35.330
In other words, when wires are tangled together, when string is tangled together,

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:37.360
the idea is that things are confusing.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:42.710
When you untangle them and you straighten it out and every string is separated

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:43.670
then things become what?

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:44.860
Clear.

00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:49.400
Untie the knot in my tongue; the first meaning of it is

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:51.550
give me clarity in speech.

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:54.760
When I speak sometimes I'm not clear

00:35:55.640 --> 00:35:58.060
when I speak sometimes everything is mixed up together.

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:01.111
This is the Du'a of Musa 'alaihissalam

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:03.031
when he was going to speak publicly, when

00:36:03.050 --> 00:36:04.490
he was going to speak in front of Fir'own.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:07.650
You know, some of you when you have to speak publicly, you get nervous.

00:36:08.230 --> 00:36:11.320
Some of you came up to the mic and you spoke and you're very brave.

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:13.240
But some of you you're thinking about coming

00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:15.550
and you're asking yourself what's going to happen.

00:36:15.930 --> 00:36:16.850
People will see me.

00:36:17.630 --> 00:36:18.670
He might say something.

00:36:18.920 --> 00:36:20.410
People might laugh.

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:21.550
I will die.

00:36:23.230 --> 00:36:24.780
Like there are a lot of questions you're asking

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:27.520
and when you do speak publicly, you get super nervous

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:28.750
and you start getting like,

00:36:29.330 --> 00:36:30.850
confused and, you know,

00:36:30.970 --> 00:36:34.250
and I used to be like that, too. I had a really hard time speaking in public.

00:36:34.830 --> 00:36:38.660
I don't have a hard time anymore because I pretend all of you are gummy bears

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:39.850
and I don't

00:36:40.240 --> 00:36:41.370
None of it--

00:36:42.030 --> 00:36:44.250
doesn't mean anything to me anymore, right? So

00:36:44.630 --> 00:36:44.960
but

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:48.690
when you are speaking publicly, you can become confused.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:50.660
Some of you do presentations for work,

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:55.320
And you have a power point presentation and you have hands-- notes in your hand,

00:36:55.320 --> 00:37:00.750
20 pages and this and that, and you go there. As soon as they put the mic on you, like uh.

00:37:01.130 --> 00:37:06.950
You have point A, B, C, D and E. You ended up doing X, W, P, K, 4, 37

00:37:07.330 --> 00:37:10.660
and came back down and say, How was it?

00:37:11.530 --> 00:37:12.960
No clarity in speech.

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:16.263
He's saying
وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِّن لِّسَانِي

00:37:16.250 --> 00:37:20.473
because he's asking, Ya Allah I want to have clear speech.

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:24.450
I want to--so anybody who hears me knows where it began and where it ended.

00:37:25.530 --> 00:37:29.460
A clear beginning, A clear middle. A clear end. It's heading in a straight line.

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:30.960
You see?

00:37:31.430 --> 00:37:33.850
This is a part of the construction of speech.

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:36.540
Sometimes, for example, you hear a Khutbah and at the end of it

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:37.750
you have no idea what just happened.

00:37:39.530 --> 00:37:41.540
You know? At least those of you that were awake,

00:37:42.730 --> 00:37:46.350
He said a lot of things, but I don't know what he actually said.

00:37:47.030 --> 00:37:50.000
And it's true. I do listen to a lot of ministers, preachers.

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:51.480
I listen to them

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:54.770
and for a lot of them you can listen to them for an hour and at the end of it

00:37:54.770 --> 00:37:56.863
if you were to ask me, what did he talk about?

00:37:56.903 --> 00:37:58.450
I'd say, I have no idea.

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:00.340
He said a lot of things, though.

00:38:01.320 --> 00:38:02.940
There's no clarity in the speech.

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:04.650
You have friends like that.

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:07.150
Your friends can talk to you for 30 minutes on the phone

00:38:09.110 --> 00:38:10.540
and at the end of it you'll say,

00:38:11.220 --> 00:38:12.650
What are you saying?

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:15.390
And you're afraid to ask.

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:17.740
Because they will try to explain to you in another 30 minutes.

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:20.540
Not say anything at all,

00:38:20.920 --> 00:38:21.640
you know?

00:38:22.220 --> 00:38:24.106
وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِّن لِّسَانِي

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:26.984
Untie the knot in my tongue. Give me clarity in my speech.

00:38:26.990 --> 00:38:28.860
It's beautiful because it actually covered the

00:38:28.860 --> 00:38:31.870
literal and the figurative together. Literally

00:38:31.880 --> 00:38:36.530
it also means that I get tongue tied. In other words, Musa 'alaihissalam had a stutter

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:39.410
so he couldn't pronounce words.

00:38:39.410 --> 00:38:42.040
You get stuck, you know, and people who have a stutter,

00:38:42.050 --> 00:38:44.290
and they have that kind of problem in their speech

00:38:44.300 --> 00:38:47.540
when they get angry, it gets worse. When they get nervous, it gets worse,

00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:50.160
and when they're under pressure, it gets worse.

00:38:50.170 --> 00:38:52.370
Musa 'alaihissalam is going to be under a lot of

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:54.600
pressure because he has to debate with me Fir'own,

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:55.280
Fir'own by the way

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:57.770
one of those savviest politicians. Can you imagine

00:38:57.770 --> 00:38:59.720
someone who has a stutter in their speech

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:01.940
is going to go on national television and

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:04.640
speak against one of the most aggressive journalists.

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:06.250
You know?

00:39:06.380 --> 00:39:08.150
This guy's gonna get slaughtered,

00:39:08.620 --> 00:39:12.340
so he's just asking like, Ya Allah, You know I have a knot in my tongue. Just give me clarity.

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:16.360
The other thing that's really important here and we'll see that on Sunday night.

00:39:16.370 --> 00:39:19.856
The explanation of you know,
وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِّن لِّسَانِي

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:23.940
if you if you talk to a really smart politician in a debate,

00:39:24.448 --> 00:39:26.450
which-- Firo'own is a really smart politician,

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:29.340
then they know how to throw you off

00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:33.660
like you came and you prepared your speech and you were going to talk about this.

00:39:33.670 --> 00:39:37.250
But they came and they came to question attack you this way, that way, the other way,

00:39:37.250 --> 00:39:37.930
the other way.

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:40.450
And you're like, Oh, what's going on? I'm confused.

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:41.650
Where do I go?

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:45.340
Sometimes you don't know where-- how to answer the questions,

00:39:45.350 --> 00:39:47.600
how to navigate them because you're just getting attacked

00:39:47.610 --> 00:39:49.250
and Fir'own is very good at attacking.

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:51.700
So even a normal person who hadn't who

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:53.690
knows what they're talking about would get confused.

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:55.750
You know this happens on television all the time, right?

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:58.360
They'll invite an academic or a scholar,

00:39:58.360 --> 00:39:59.930
someone who knows what they're talking about.

00:39:59.940 --> 00:40:04.030
But the host of the show, like in America, we have the Fox TV channel, right?

00:40:04.710 --> 00:40:07.490
You have the host who will attack this guy from every angle

00:40:07.490 --> 00:40:09.510
and even though this guy knows what he's talking about,

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:12.240
he got confused and he looked stupid in front of everyone.

00:40:12.910 --> 00:40:14.940
And they're very good at making you look bad.

00:40:15.510 --> 00:40:16.640
Thye're very good at that.

00:40:17.110 --> 00:40:19.150
Fir'own is going to be very good at that,

00:40:19.240 --> 00:40:21.730
and Musa 'alaihissalam is going to walk into his court

00:40:22.110 --> 00:40:25.140
on his terms, and he's going to have to deal with him directly.

00:40:25.170 --> 00:40:27.240
That's not gonna be easy. So he asked Allah, Ya Allah,

00:40:27.710 --> 00:40:29.210
I need clarity in my speech.

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:30.640
I can't afford to stutter

00:40:31.310 --> 00:40:34.840
because if I stutter even a little bit he'll say, What happened? You can't talk.

00:40:34.850 --> 00:40:37.240
You can't answer my question. You got nervous. What's going on?

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:41.040
Why can't you do it? And he actually did it, Musa 'alaihissalam was speaking with him.

00:40:41.050 --> 00:40:41.630
And he said,

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:43.370
This guy

00:40:43.389 --> 00:40:47.820
(وَلا يَكَادُ يُبِينُ) he can't even clearly speak. Look at him. Look at him stuttering.

00:40:47.830 --> 00:40:49.640
He's gonna debate with me?

00:40:50.210 --> 00:40:53.530
That's literally what you-- imagine that happening to you in a in a conversation.

00:40:54.110 --> 00:40:55.520
Hey what's the matter with you? You can't talk?

00:40:56.010 --> 00:40:56.420
huh?

00:40:56.810 --> 00:40:57.320
Oh,

00:40:59.410 --> 00:41:01.440
This is what Fir'own is doing to Musa 'alaihissalam

00:41:02.110 --> 00:41:04.256
this is my one of my favorite Du'as actually,

00:41:04.376 --> 00:41:06.296
وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِّن لِّسَانِي.  يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي

00:41:06.310 --> 00:41:09.010
Why? Because this Du'a

00:41:09.310 --> 00:41:11.020
helped me in public speaking.

00:41:12.310 --> 00:41:15.240
My teacher told me, Use this Du'a when you're about to go give a talk,

00:41:15.710 --> 00:41:16.330
I do it every time.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:18.870
And when I don't do it, you can tell.

00:41:19.140 --> 00:41:20.630
Nobody knows what I talked about.

00:41:21.710 --> 00:41:23.970
Nobody knows. The first time I went to speak,

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:25.520
I had these notes prepared

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:28.930
five pages of notes for a 15 minute Khutbah.

00:41:30.210 --> 00:41:34.640
I was gonna, you know, and I got up there and the words on the page crawled away.

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:39.600
I'm like, holding this thing like, uh, and Allah subhanahu wa ta'ala

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:44.530
also says, where is it? Where is it? Where does He say it? Where does He say it?

00:41:45.710 --> 00:41:47.880
And at the end of the Khutbah and the Salah

00:41:48.090 --> 00:41:50.360
I made a mistake in the Fatiha. I was that nervous.

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:52.740
I started with the Surah.

00:41:53.310 --> 00:41:55.040
They corrected me
الحمد لله

00:41:55.280 --> 00:41:57.720
(الحمد لله) Oh, yeah (الحمد لله)

00:41:59.510 --> 00:42:00.300
It was horrible.

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:03.640
And when the Khutbah was done, people came up to me and patted me on the shoulder.

00:42:03.640 --> 00:42:04.240
It's okay.

00:42:04.610 --> 00:42:05.330
It's okay.

00:42:07.710 --> 00:42:09.400
That was like a condolence

00:42:09.570 --> 00:42:11.100
at the end of my first Khutbah,

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:12.530
you know?

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:15.766
But then I realized the value of
وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِّن لِّسَانِي

00:42:15.770 --> 00:42:18.526
It's the Du'a of confidence.  It's the Du'a of confidence.

00:42:18.576 --> 00:42:23.010
Okay, so now I'm going to give you your next break.